// The low and high end of the single-byte range. The input carries the NUL byte 0x00, a tab, and DEL 0x7F, which is
// the last code point UTF-8 encodes in a single byte. A backend which treats the input as C strings stops at the NUL,
// and one which uses a signed byte type puts 0x7F next to the multi-byte range instead of at the end of the
// single-byte one.
//
// NUL and CONTROL both match the NUL byte with one byte each, so the declaration order decides it, the same way
// rule-order does for two ordinary rules.
//
// The parser is not what this case is about, so the grammar is the single empty production the file format requires.

@scanner {
    NUL:     /\0/;
    CONTROL: /[[:cntrl:]]/;
    LETTER:  /[a-z]/;
}

@parser {
    tokens
        : @empty
        ;
}
